fix(hook): make the guard's check entry point say when it did not understand (#287)

`check` takes the git arguments WITHOUT the `git` word. Handed the word it read
`git` as the subcommand, matched nothing destructive and exited 0 in silence --
measured against the guard as it stood, in this checkout:

  check -- clean -fd     exit 2   refused, naming the run and the path
  check git clean -fd    exit 0   silent, 0 bytes of stderr

Nothing was unprotected: `hook` and `command` both refused that command, and
they are the two live callers. `check` is the third entry point and the only one
with no caller yet -- documented as "the decision alone, on an argv, for anything
else that wants it" -- so it is the contract every future caller reads, and it
answered a caller that got the form slightly wrong exactly as it answers a safe
command. That is a control that does not act and does not say so.

TWO SHAPES ARE NOT AN ARGV and both were a silent allow: one naming no git
subcommand at all (empty, a bare `--`, global options alone), and one token
carrying spaces, which is a whole shell command line handed to the mode that
takes an argv. Both now exit 3 and say so on stderr. 3 is NOT a refusal -- the
file's contract is that only 2 refuses and a caller allows on anything else --
and it exists so the answer can be told apart from the 0 that means safe.

THE PROGRAM WORD IS ACCEPTED AND SKIPPED rather than diagnosed, because git has
no `git` subcommand, so a leading `git` can only be the program word; answering
correctly beats answering "I did not understand you". The cost is in the file:
`check git git clean -fd` is weighed as `git clean -fd`.

The rule is a SHAPE and not a list of git subcommands. A list here would be a
second spelling of git's own and would drift; a subcommand is one word.

`hook` IS UNCHANGED, deliberately. Its header says nothing is guessed from a
command it cannot tokenise, and that is right: it receives a shell STRING it may
legitimately fail to parse, and guessing there would refuse commands that were
never destructive. `check` receives an ARGV a caller chose. `wrap` is unchanged
too and must be -- installed as `git` its argv is a command line a human typed,
and stripping a word there would refuse `git git clean -fd`, which real git
rejects harmlessly.

